# Citation File Format 1.2.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# What this file is for
# ---------------------
# GitHub reads it to render "Cite this repository", and Zenodo reads it to fill
# the metadata of a deposit when a release is published from a tag. Both
# conversions are automatic; the BibTeX and APA that GitHub offers are generated
# from here, which is why this file and not a hand-written CITATION.bib.
#
# The rule this file lives under: `version` and `authors` must agree with
# pyproject.toml and with src/quoss/__init__.py, and
# tests/unit/test_project_config.py fails when they do not. Three places that
# name the same version is two places that can drift, so the drift is asserted
# rather than remembered.
#
# Releasing a new version is four steps and they are written down in
# CHANGELOG.md, not here: this file is the metadata, not the procedure.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "QuOSS — Quantum Optical Satellite Simulator"
message: >-
  If you use this software, or report a figure it produced, please cite it
  using the metadata below. Please cite the version you ran: the physics
  changes between versions, and `quoss --version` prints the version and the
  commit of whatever produced your numbers.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Adrià
    family-names: Sancho
    email: francesc.adria.sancho@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/W1Adri/QuOSS"
abstract: >-
  A vectorised simulator of quantum optical links, satellite-to-ground and
  ground-to-ground, that answers how many secret key bits a link certifies
  under a composable finite-key bound rather than asymptotically. It covers
  orbital geometry, the turbulent atmospheric channel, decoy-state BB84, and
  the system metrics built on them, from a versioned YAML scenario whose hash
  travels with every result. Its validation table records, for thirty-five
  cases from eight published sources, what was compared, under which derived
  tolerance, and what came out — including the eight comparisons that do not
  reproduce.
keywords:
  - quantum key distribution
  - satellite communications
  - free-space optical link
  - atmospheric turbulence
  - finite-key analysis
  - simulation
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-19"
